The final match of the [[2018 FIFA World Cup]], the 21st edition of [[FIFA]]'s competition for national [[association football|football]] teams, was played at the [[Luzhniki Stadium]] in [[Moscow]], Russia, on 15 July 2018, and was contested by [[France national football team|France]] and [[Croatia national football team|Croatia]]. 

The tournament comprised hosts [[Russia national football team|Russia]] and 31 other teams who emerged from [[2018 FIFA World Cup qualification|the qualification phase]], organised by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]]. The 32 teams competed in a group stage, from which 16 teams qualified for [[2018 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|the knockout stage]]. En route to the final, France finished first in [[2018 FIFA World Cup Group C|Group C]], with two wins and a draw, after which they defeated [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] in the round of 16, [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]] in the quarter-final and [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]] in the semi-final. Croatia finished first in [[2018 FIFA World Cup Group D|Group D]] with three wins, before defeating [[Denmark national football team|Denmark]] in the round of 16 and Russia in the quarter-final – both through a [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] – and then [[England national football team|England]] in the semi-final. The final took place in front of 78,011 supporters, with more than 1.1 billion watching on television, and was [[Referee (association football)|refereed]] by [[Néstor Pitana]] from Argentina.

France took the lead through a controversial free kick that led to an [[own goal]] by [[Mario Mandžukić]] in the 18th minute – the first own goal in a World Cup final – before [[Ivan Perišić]] equalised 10 minutes later with a low shot into the corner of the goal. Shortly afterwards, France were awarded a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] when Pitana ruled that Perišić had deliberately handled the ball. The decision resulted from a consultation with the [[video assistant referee]], which was in use for the first time in a World Cup at the 2018 tournament. The decision was criticised by some pundits such as [[Alan Shearer]], who labelled it "ridiculous", while others such as [[Chris Waddle]] thought it was correct. [[Antoine Griezmann]] scored to give France a 2–1 half-time lead. They extended that lead in the 59th minute, when [[Paul Pogba]] scored at the second attempt after Croatia defender [[Dejan Lovren]] had blocked his initial shot. [[Kylian Mbappé]] scored from outside the penalty area to make it 4–1, becoming the second teenager to score in a World Cup final (after [[Pelé]] in [[1958 FIFA World Cup final|1958]]). In the 69th minute, Mandžukić capitalized on a [[Hugo Lloris]] error to score a second for Croatia. The final score was 4–2 to France.

France's win was their [[List of FIFA World Cup finals|second World Cup title]], following their victory [[1998 FIFA World Cup final|in 1998]], which they hosted. Griezmann was named the [[man of the match]], while Croatia's [[Luka Modrić]] was awarded the [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Ball|Golden Ball]] as FIFA's outstanding player of the tournament. The final was the highest-scoring World Cup final since [[1966 FIFA World Cup final|1966]] and the only final since [[2002 FIFA World Cup final|2002]] to end in regulation time.

==Background==
[[File:Adidas Telstar Mechta Ball.jpg|thumb|right|An example of the Adidas Telstar Mechta ball used in the match]]
The [[2018 FIFA World Cup]] was the 21st edition of the [[FIFA World Cup|World Cup]], [[FIFA]]'s football competition for national teams, held in Russia between 14 June and 15 July 2018.<ref name="Britanica">{{Cite encyclopedia |url-status=live |title=World Cup: History & Winners |last1=Tikkanen |first1=Amy |last2=Augustyn |first2=Adam |last3=Levy |first3=Michael |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210828023709/https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football |archive-date=28 August 2021 |last4=Ray |first4=Michael |last5=Luebering |first5=J. E. |last6=Lotha |first6=Gloria |last7=Young |first7=Grace |last8=Shepherd |first8=Melinda C. |last9=Sinha |first9=Surabhi |last10=Rodriguez |first10=Emily |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopædia Britannica]] |date=19 August 2021 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football }}</ref><ref name="RSSSF_summary">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2018 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=9 August 2018 |access-date=17 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2018f.html |archive-date=30 June 2020 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200630070249/http://rsssf.com/tables/2018f.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Russia national football team|Russia]] qualified for the finals automatically as tournament hosts, while 208 teams competed for the remaining 31 spots through [[2018 FIFA World Cup qualification|qualifying rounds]] organised by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]] and held between June 2015 and November 2017.<ref name="FifaQualif">{{Cite web |title=The Qualifiers in Numbers |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=16 November 2017 |access-date=17 October 2021 |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2018russia/news/the-qualifiers-in-numbers-2920457 |archive-date=17 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211017131537/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2018russia/news/the-qualifiers-in-numbers-2920457 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2018 Qualifying |last2=Stokkermans |first2=Karel |last1=King |first1=Ian |date=5 July 2018 |access-date=17 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2018q.html |archive-date=16 November 2017 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171116132532/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2018q.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In the finals, the teams were divided into eight groups of four with each team playing each other once in a [[round-robin tournament|round-robin]] format. The two top teams from each group advanced to a [[Single-elimination tournament|knock-out phase]].<ref name="RSSSF_summary"/> The defending [[2014 FIFA World Cup final|champions]] from [[2014 FIFA World Cup|the 2014 World Cup]] were [[Germany national football team|Germany]].<ref name="2014f">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2014 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=5 July 2018 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2014f.html |archive-date=19 October 2018 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181019014641/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2014f.html |url-status=live }}</ref> They were eliminated in the group phase at the 2018 event, however, finishing bottom of [[2018 FIFA World Cup Group F|their group]] behind [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]], [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]] and [[South Korea national football team|South Korea]].<ref name="RSSSF_summary"/>

[[France national football team|France]] had won the World Cup once before as hosts, beating [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] in the [[1998 FIFA World Cup final|1998 final]] at the [[Stade de France]]. They had also finished in second place once before, losing to [[Italy national football team|Italy]] in the [[2006 FIFA World Cup final|2006 final]] via penalties.<ref name="Britanica"/> The team had reached the quarter-final of the previous World Cup,<ref name="2014f"/> and then finished as [[UEFA Euro 2016 Final|runners-up]] to [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]] as hosts at [[Euro 2016]].<ref name="SI-FRA">{{cite magazine |date=1 June 2018 |title=Loaded, Deep France Enters World Cup as One of Favorites to Win it All |url=https://www.si.com/soccer/2018/06/01/2018-fifa-world-cup-team-preview-analysing-frances-chances-tournament |magazine=[[Sports Illustrated]] |access-date=10 July 2018 |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711021829/https://www.si.com/soccer/2018/06/01/2018-fifa-world-cup-team-preview-analysing-frances-chances-tournament |url-status=live }}</ref> 

The 2018 final was the first for [[Croatia national football team|Croatia]] in their fifth World Cup appearance. They became the tenth European country and thirteenth overall to reach a World Cup final.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://digitalhub.fifa.com/m/36eb0f9e452467ef/original/trosebngotecouavz6xf-pdf.pdf |title=Stats of the day – 12 July 2018 |date=12 July 2018 |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |page=1 |access-date=12 July 2018 |archive-date=27 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211027084542/https://digitalhub.fifa.com/m/36eb0f9e452467ef/original/trosebngotecouavz6xf-pdf.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-worldcup-cro-eng/mandzukic-sends-croatia-to-first-world-cup-final-idUSKBN1K12QC |title=Mandzukic sends irrepressible Croatia into first World Cup final |last=Phillips |first=Mitch |date=11 July 2018 |access-date=12 July 2018 |publisher=[[Reuters]] |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711210448/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-worldcup-cro-eng/mandzukic-sends-croatia-to-first-world-cup-final-idUSKBN1K12QC |url-status=live }}</ref> Their previous best performance was at the 1998 World Cup, where they reached the semi-final before losing to France.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/preview-france-v-croatia |title=Preview: France v Croatia |date=14 July 2018 |access-date=14 July 2018 |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |archive-date=14 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180714092803/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/preview-france-v-croatia |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/07/11/sports/croatia-world-cup-history.html |title=Croatia's 2018 World Cup Pursuit Inspired by the Past |last=McAleavy |first=Emma L. |date=11 July 2018 |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |archive-date=12 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180712040206/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/07/11/sports/croatia-world-cup-history.html |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.ie/sport/soccer/world-cup-2018/fixtures-and-results/croatia-at-the-1998-world-cup-a-look-back-at-their-last-semifinal-run-37100133.html |title=Croatia at the 1998 World Cup – a look back at their last semi-final run |date=10 July 2018 |work=[[Irish Independent]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |agency=Press Association |archive-date=16 November 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181116211117/https://www.independent.ie/sport/soccer/world-cup-2018/fixtures-and-results/croatia-at-the-1998-world-cup-a-look-back-at-their-last-semifinal-run-37100133.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Croatia had been eliminated in the group stage at the 2014 tournament,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285326 |title=Croatia 1–3 Mexico |date=23 June 2014 |access-date=13 July 2018 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |archive-date=21 December 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141221193132/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/25285326 |url-status=live }}</ref> before reaching the round of 16 at Euro 2016, where they lost to eventual-winners Portugal.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2016e.html | access-date = 24 September 2021 | title = European Championship 2016 | date = 15 October 2020 | first = Karel | last = Stokkermans | archive-date = 12 June 2021 | website = [[RSSSF]] | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210612002331/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2016e.html | url-status = live }}</ref> The final was the sixth meeting between the two teams, with France undefeated in the previous fixtures with three wins and two draws.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Croatia national football team: record v France |publisher=[[AFS Enterprises]] |work=11v11 |date= |access-date=18 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/croatia/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/France/ |archive-date=18 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211018163346/https://www.11v11.com/teams/croatia/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/France/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Croatia's team at the time was described by many pundits as a [[golden generation]], with [[Luka Modrić]], [[Ivan Rakitić]] and [[Mario Mandžukić]] as the key players, although with many members of the team having passed the age of thirty, the 2018 World Cup was described as "surely a last quest towards making an impact on the greatest stage" by ''[[The Guardian]]''{{'s}} Shaun Walker.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Croatia's Luka Modric: 'It is only right there are great expectations of us' |last=Walker |first=Shaun |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=16 June 2018 |access-date=19 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/16/croatia-luka-modric-great-expectations |archive-date=19 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211019215131/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/16/croatia-luka-modric-great-expectations |url-status=live }}</ref> The final was the ninth all-European World Cup final, the most recent having been in [[2010 FIFA World Cup final|2010]] when [[Spain national football team|Spain]] won 1–0 against the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/magic-moments-in-all-european-finals |title=Magic moments in all-European Finals |date=12 July 2018 |access-date=14 July 2018 |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |archive-date=13 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180713221140/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/magic-moments-in-all-european-finals |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesw/worldcup.html |title=World Cup 1930–2018 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=5 July 2018 |access-date=10 July 2018 |archive-date=24 March 2019 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190324122350/http://www.rsssf.com/tablesw/worldCup.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

France and Croatia had played each other twice at major tournaments before. Their first such meeting was the [[1998 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#France vs Croatia|1998 World Cup semi-final]] at the Stade de France, where [[Davor Šuker]] opened the scoring for Croatia but France came back to win 2–1 courtesy of [[Lilian Thuram]]'s only two goals for France in his international career. It also featured the only red card of [[Laurent Blanc]]'s career. The second such meeting was in [[UEFA Euro 2004 Group B#Croatia vs France|Group B of UEFA Euro 2004]], and the two nations tied 2–2 in Leiria.

The official match ball for the final was the Telstar Mechta ({{langx|ru|Мечта}}; {{translation}} dream or ambition), a red-coloured variant of the [[Adidas Telstar 18]] introduced for the knockout stage.<ref>{{cite news |last=Wright |first=Chris |date=26 June 2018 |title=World Cup 2018 match ball released – yep, that's right, another one |url=http://www.espn.com/soccer/blog/the-toe-poke/65/post/3544878/world-cup-2018-match-ball-released-yep-thats-right-another-one |publisher=[[ESPN]] |access-date=14 July 2018 |archive-date=14 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180714221805/http://www.espn.com/soccer/blog/the-toe-poke/65/post/3544878/world-cup-2018-match-ball-released-yep-thats-right-another-one |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |date=26 June 2018 |title=adidas Football Reveals Official Match Ball for the Knockout Stage of the 2018 FIFA World Cup Russia |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/adidas-football-reveals-official-match-ball-for-the-knockout-stage-of-the-2018-f |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |access-date=14 July 2018 |archive-date=26 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180626192447/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/adidas-football-reveals-official-match-ball-for-the-knockout-stage-of-the-2018-f |url-status=live }}</ref> The Telstar family, a homage to the original [[Adidas Telstar|1970 Telstar]], was designed similarly to 2014's [[Brazuca]], but with longer seams and additional panels.<ref>{{cite news |last=Sabin |first=Dyani |date=18 June 2018 |title=How the new World Cup ball was designed to not influence the games |url=https://www.popsci.com/world-cup-ball-design |work=[[Popular Science]] |access-date=14 July 2018 |archive-date=14 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180714044150/https://www.popsci.com/world-cup-ball-design |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Venue==
The final was played at the [[Luzhniki Stadium]] in [[Moscow]], located in the [[Khamovniki District]] of the [[Central Administrative Okrug]]. An expanded version of the stadium was named as the provisional final venue in Russia's [[Russia 2018 FIFA World Cup bid|World Cup bid]], which was selected by FIFA on 2 December 2010.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748703377504575650434108578688 |title=Russia to Host 2018 World Cup; Qatar Gets 2022 |last1=Clegg |first1=Jonathan |date=2 December 2010 |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |access-date=10 July 2018 |last2=White |first2=Gregory L. |url-access=subscription |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711035836/https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748703377504575650434108578688 |url-status=live }}</ref> Luzhniki Stadium was confirmed as the final venue on 14 December 2012, following a meeting of the FIFA Executive Committee held in [[Tokyo]], Japan.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2012/m=12/news=russia-2018-start-and-finish-luzhniki-stadium-1971808.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150403100626/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2012/m=12/news=russia-2018-start-and-finish-luzhniki-stadium-1971808.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=3 April 2015 |title=Russia 2018 to start and finish at Luzhniki Stadium |date=15 December 2012 |access-date=17 November 2017 |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com}}</ref> The stadium also hosted six other matches, including the opening match on 14 June, three group stage matches, a round of 16 match, and the second semi-final match.<ref name="FIFA-Facts2">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/luzhniki-stadium-all-you-need-to-know-2927040 |title=Luzhniki Stadium: All you need to know |date=26 January 2016 |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |access-date=10 July 2018 |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711041703/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/luzhniki-stadium-all-you-need-to-know-2927040 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="match schedule2">{{cite web |url=https://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/66/71/62/2018fwc_matchschedule_20122017_en_neutral.pdf |title=FIFA World Cup Russia 2018 – Match Schedule |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |access-date=20 December 2017 |archive-date=24 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180624041527/https://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/66/71/62/2018fwc_matchschedule_20122017_en_neutral.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref>

[[File:Luzhniki Stadium1.jpg|thumb|The exterior of the Luzhniki Stadium at night with [[Main building of Moscow State University|Moscow State University]] in the background]]
The Luzhniki Stadium, previously known as the Grand Arena of the Central Lenin Stadium until 1992, originally opened in 1956 as part of the [[Luzhniki Olympic Complex]] to host the [[Spartakiad|USSR Summer Spartakiade]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://uk.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-world-russia-2018-insight/putins-goal-is-to-showcase-russia-at-2018-world-cup-idUKKBN0FJ0QN20140714 |title=Putin's goal is to showcase Russia at 2018 World Cup |date=14 July 2014 |access-date=10 July 2018 |publisher=[[Reuters]] |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711025308/https://uk.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-world-russia-2018-insight/putins-goal-is-to-showcase-russia-at-2018-world-cup-idUKKBN0FJ0QN20140714 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="1980Olympics2">{{cite report |url=https://library.olympic.org/Default/doc/SYRACUSE/46841/games-of-the-xxii-olympiad-official-report-of-the-organising-committee-of-the-games-of-the-xxii-olym |title=Games of the XXII Olympiad: Official Report of the Organising Committee, Volume 2 |author=[[Fizkultura i sport|Moscow Fiscultura i Sport]] |publisher=[[International Olympic Committee]] |pages=46–51 |access-date=10 July 2018 |year=1981 |via=Olympics World Library |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711064732/https://library.olympic.org/Default/doc/SYRACUSE/46841/games-of-the-xxii-olympiad-official-report-of-the-organising-committee-of-the-games-of-the-xxii-olym |url-status=live }}</ref> It served as the [[national stadium]] of the country, hosting many matches for the Russia national team and its predecessor, the [[Soviet Union national football team|Soviet Union national team]].<ref name="FIFA-Facts2"/><ref name="FIFA-Stadium2">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/destination/stadiums/stadium=810/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151118045214/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/destination/stadiums/stadium=810/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=18 November 2015 |title=Luzhniki Stadium |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com|access-date=17 November 2017}}</ref> In the past, it was the home ground at various times for [[PFC CSKA Moscow|CSKA Moscow]], [[Torpedo Moscow]], and [[FC Spartak Moscow|Spartak Moscow]]. However, {{as of|2017|lc=yes}}, there are currently no clubs based at the stadium.<ref name="FIFA-Stadium2"/><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/destination/cities/city=1559/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150202095836/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/destination/cities/city=1559/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=2 February 2015 |title=Moscow |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |access-date=17 November 2017}}</ref>

Rated as a [[UEFA stadium categories|category 4]] stadium by [[UEFA]], the Luzhniki Stadium was the largest at the 2018 World Cup; it usually had a maximum capacity of 81,006, but was reduced to 78,011 for the World Cup.<ref name="FIFA-Stadium2"/><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.espn.com/soccer/fifa-world-cup/4/blog/post/3291823/russia-world-cup-2018-stadium-venues-12-grounds-across-11-cities |title=Russia 2018 World Cup stadium venues: 12 grounds across 11 cities |date=1 December 2017 |access-date=10 July 2018 |publisher=[[ESPN]] Internet Ventures |agency=[[Press Association]] |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711042238/http://www.espn.com/soccer/fifa-world-cup/4/blog/post/3291823/russia-world-cup-2018-stadium-venues-12-grounds-across-11-cities |url-status=live }}</ref> This also makes the stadium the largest in Eastern Europe,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2275704-top-100-stadiums-in-world-football |title=Top 100 Stadiums in World Football |last=Tighe |first=Sam |date=18 December 2014 |publisher=[[Bleacher Report]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |archive-date=12 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180712002717/https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2275704-top-100-stadiums-in-world-football |url-status=live }}</ref> and [[List of European stadiums by capacity|among the largest in Europe]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://archexplore.com/2018/06/03/luzhniki-stadium-architecture/ |title=Luzhniki Stadium – Russia World Cup Venue 2018 |date=3 June 2018 |website=ArchExplore |access-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711063949/http://archexplore.com/2018/06/03/luzhniki-stadium-architecture/ |archive-date=11 July 2018 |url-status=dead }}</ref> To prepare for the World Cup, the stadium was closed for extensive renovations in August 2013.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.jamaicaobserver.com/sport/Luzhniki-Stadium-for-major-renovation-after-World-Champs_14828199 |title=Luzhniki Stadium for major renovation after World Champs |date=5 August 2013 |work=[[Jamaica Observer]] |access-date=10 July 2018 |agency=[[Agence France-Presse]] |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711033601/http://www.jamaicaobserver.com/sport/Luzhniki-Stadium-for-major-renovation-after-World-Champs_14828199 |url-status=live }}</ref> The spectator stands were moved closer to the pitch, which was converted from artificial turf to natural grass, after the removal of the athletic track. The historic facade of the stadium was preserved due to its architectural value, while the roof was upgraded using a new [[polycarbonate]] skin with exterior lighting.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://tass.com/sport/993250 |title=Experts name Luzhniki Stadium in Moscow as 2017 world's best football arena |date=7 March 2018 |access-date=10 July 2018 |agency=[[TASS]] |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711022405/http://tass.com/sport/993250 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.mos.ru/en/news/item/22384073/ |title=Luzhniki Stadium almost finished |date=31 March 2017 |publisher=Government of Moscow |access-date=10 July 2018 |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711022618/https://www.mos.ru/en/news/item/22384073/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The Luzhniki did not host any matches at the [[2017 FIFA Confederations Cup]] due to the ongoing project.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/2017/06/12/russian-stadiums-readying-to-host-confederations-cup/102773208/ |title=Russian stadiums readying to host Confederations Cup |date=12 June 2017 |work=[[USA Today]] |access-date=10 July 2018 |agency=[[Associated Press]] |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711044550/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/2017/06/12/russian-stadiums-readying-to-host-confederations-cup/102773208/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The renovation project cost €341&nbsp;million,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.dawn.com/news/1355080 |title=Luzhniki Stadium's renovation completed |date=31 August 2017 |work=[[Dawn (newspaper)|Dawn]] |access-date=10 July 2018 |agency=Agence France-Presse |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711025559/https://www.dawn.com/news/1355080 |url-status=live }}</ref> and the stadium officially reopened with an international [[Friendly match|friendly]] between Russia and [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] on 11 November 2017.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.efe.com/efe/english/sports/argentina-beats-russia-1-0-in-friendly-at-renovated-luzhniki-stadium/50000266-3435513 |title=Argentina beats Russia 1–0 in friendly at renovated Luzhniki Stadium |date=11 November 2017 |access-date=10 July 2018 |publisher=[[EFE]] |archive-date=30 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200930080507/https://www.efe.com/efe/english/sports/argentina-beats-russia-1-0-in-friendly-at-renovated-luzhniki-stadium/50000266-3435513 |url-status=live }}</ref>

