France entered the 2018 World Cup as the [[bookmaker]]s' favourites to win the tournament,<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Euro 2016 betting guide |last=Carbis |first=Ian |work=WalesOnline |publisher=[[Media Wales]] |date=1 June 2016 |access-date=18 October 2021 |url=https://www.walesonline.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/euro-2016-betting-odds-latest-11444250 |archive-date=18 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211018164248/https://www.walesonline.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/euro-2016-betting-odds-latest-11444250 |url-status=live }}</ref> and qualified for the tournament by finishing first in their [[2018 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group A|qualification group]], ahead of Sweden and the Netherlands.<ref name="SI-FRA"/> In the finals, they were drawn in [[2018 FIFA World Cup Group C|Group C]] alongside [[Australia men's national soccer team|Australia]], [[Denmark national football team|Denmark]], and [[Peru national football team|Peru]].<ref name="RSSSF_summary"/> Their opening match was against Australia at [[Kazan Arena]] in [[Kazan]] on 16 June.<ref>{{Cite web |title=France deny Australia amid VAR controversy – in pictures |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=16 June 2018 |access-date=18 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/gallery/2018/jun/16/world-cup-2018-france-deny-australia-amid-var-controversy-in-pictures |archive-date=18 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211018171434/https://www.theguardian.com/football/gallery/2018/jun/16/world-cup-2018-france-deny-australia-amid-var-controversy-in-pictures |url-status=live }}</ref> France won the game 2–1 with [[Antoine Griezmann]] scoring the first goal from a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] called by the [[video assistant referee]]. This was followed by a penalty for Australia which was scored by [[Mile Jedinak]], before France scored the winner through an [[own goal]] deflected in by Australian defender [[Aziz Behich]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Sutcliffe |first=Steve |date=18 June 2018 |title=France 2–1 Australia |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44422186 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=10 July 2018 |archive-date=16 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180616041515/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44422186 |url-status=live }}</ref> France's second match was against Peru at [[Central Stadium (Yekaterinburg)|Central Stadium]] in [[Yekaterinburg]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=France 1 Peru 0 |publisher=[[Sky Sports]] |date=21 June 2018 |access-date=18 October 2021 |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/france-vs-peru/video/385185 |archive-date=18 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211018173145/https://www.skysports.com/football/france-vs-peru/video/385185 |url-status=live }}</ref> France won 1–0 through a goal scored by 19-year-old [[Kylian Mbappé]], who became the country's youngest goalscorer at a major tournament.<ref>{{cite news |last=Clarey |first=Cristopher |date=21 June 2018 |title=36 Years and No World Cup Goals: Peru Eliminated by France |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/21/sports/world-cup/france-vs-peru.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=10 July 2018 |archive-date=10 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180710170525/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/21/sports/world-cup/france-vs-peru.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Lawrence |first=Amy |date=21 June 2018 |title=France seal last-16 spot as Kylian Mbappé earns victory over Peru |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/21/france-peru-world-cup-group-c-match-report |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=10 July 2018 |archive-date=10 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180710225254/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/21/france-peru-world-cup-group-c-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> The victory qualified France for the knockout stage, allowing [[Didier Deschamps]], the French [[Manager (association football)|manager]], to rest several starting players for the final group stage match against Denmark. That game, which took place at the Luzhniki Stadium, finished in a scoreless draw and was enough for France to win the group.<ref name="RSSSF_summary"/><ref>{{cite news |last=Dunbar |first=Graham |date=26 June 2018 |title=Denmark advances at World Cup in drab 0–0 draw with France |url=http://www.chicagotribune.com/sports/soccer/ct-spt-world-cup-denmark-france-20180626-story.html |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |agency=[[Associated Press]] |access-date=10 July 2018 |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711021844/http://www.chicagotribune.com/sports/soccer/ct-spt-world-cup-denmark-france-20180626-story.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the round of 16, France faced Group D runners-up Argentina, on 30 June at Kazan Stadium. In a match dubbed by writers for ''[[The Week]]'' as "the classic of Kazan", France won 4–3.<ref>{{cite web |title=France 4 Argentina 3: Mbappe stars in the classic of Kazan – how Twitter reacted |work=[[The Week]] |url=https://www.theweek.co.uk/2018-world-cup/94687/france-4-argentina-3-mbappe-stars-in-kazan-how-twitter-reacted |access-date=19 October 2021 |date=30 June 2018 |archive-date=8 October 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181008093749/http://www.theweek.co.uk/2018-world-cup/94687/france-4-argentina-3-mbappe-stars-in-kazan-how-twitter-reacted |url-status=live }}</ref> Griezmann gave France the lead early in the game with a penalty, after [[Marcos Rojo]] had [[Foul (association football)|fouled]] Mbappé as he ran with the ball into the [[penalty area]]. Argentina then scored on either side of half-time through [[Ángel Di María]] and [[Gabriel Mercado]] to take the lead. However, in the 57th minute, [[Benjamin Pavard]] shot a half-volley from outside the penalty area of 21 yards; to the top-left corner to brought France level again. Pavard's goal was then later voted as the best goal of the tournament. Mbappé then scored twice in four minutes and 40 seconds in the 64th and 68th minute to put them 4–2 up before [[Sergio Agüero]] scored a late third for Argentina in stoppage time. Patrick Jennings of [[BBC Sport]] described Mbappé's contribution as a "brilliant performance that will linger long in the memory".<ref>{{cite web |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |title=France 4–3 Argentina |first=Patrick |last=Jennings |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44439354 |date=30 June 2018 |access-date=19 October 2021 |archive-date=3 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180703160605/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44439354 |url-status=live }}</ref> France's quarter-final was against [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]] at [[Nizhny Novgorod Stadium]] on 6 July. They won 2–0, with goals from [[Raphaël Varane]] and Griezmann.<ref>{{Cite web |title=France beat Uruguay to reach semis |last=Bevan |first=Chris |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=6 July 2018 |access-date=19 October 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44652179 |quote=Raphael Varane |archive-date=19 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211019212125/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44652179 |url-status=live }}</ref> The team advanced to a semi-final match against [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]] at the [[Krestovsky Stadium]] in [[Saint Petersburg]] on 10 July. The game ended in a 1–0 win for the French, the winner headed into the goal by defender [[Samuel Umtiti]] following a [[corner kick]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Taylor |first=Daniel |date=10 July 2018 |title=Samuel Umtiti header puts France in World Cup final with win over Belgium |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jul/10/france-belgium-world-cup-semi-final-match-report |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=10 July 2018 |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711033527/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jul/10/france-belgium-world-cup-semi-final-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref>

