In their [[2018 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group I|qualification group]], Croatia finished second to [[Iceland national football team|Iceland]] and had to enter the [[2018 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA second round|qualifying play-offs]]. There, they faced [[Greece national football team|Greece]], whom they beat over a [[two-legged tie]], winning the first leg 4–1 and drawing 0–0 in the second.<ref name="SI-CRO2">{{cite magazine |url=https://www.si.com/soccer/2018/06/02/2018-fifa-world-cup-team-preview-analysing-croatias-chances-tournament |title=Modric, Rakitic Key Croatia's Star-Laden Squad in Difficult World Cup Group |date=2 June 2018 |magazine=[[Sports Illustrated]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |archive-date=12 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180712022359/https://www.si.com/soccer/2018/06/02/2018-fifa-world-cup-team-preview-analysing-croatias-chances-tournament |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/croatia-the-world-cups-russian-nesting-dolls-1530869400 |title=Croatia: The World Cup's Russian Nesting Dolls |last=Clegg |first=Jonathan |date=6 July 2018 |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |url-access=subscription |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711202548/https://www.wsj.com/articles/croatia-the-world-cups-russian-nesting-dolls-1530869400 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/41956892 |title=Croatia secure World Cup 2018 place with victory over Greece |date=12 November 2017 |access-date=11 July 2018 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |archive-date=10 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180910211248/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/41956892 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the finals, Croatia were drawn into [[2018 FIFA World Cup Group D|Group D]] with Argentina, Iceland, and [[Nigeria national football team|Nigeria]], considered a difficult draw due to Argentina's talent and Nigeria's historic performances.<ref name="SI-CRO2"/><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-worldcup-cro-prospects/croatia-out-to-match-success-of-1998-world-cup-generation-idUSKCN1IN2GZ |title=Croatia out to match success of 1998 World Cup generation |date=22 May 2018 |access-date=11 July 2018 |publisher=[[Reuters]] |archive-date=12 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180712041023/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-worldcup-cro-prospects/croatia-out-to-match-success-of-1998-world-cup-generation-idUSKCN1IN2GZ |url-status=live }}</ref> In their opening match, the team earned a 2–0 victory over Nigeria, with an own goal by [[Oghenekaro Etebo]] caused by Mario Mandžukić and a penalty scored by Luka Modrić.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/soccer-insider/wp/2018/06/16/croatia-vs-nigeria-2018-world-cup/ |title=Croatia vs. Nigeria 2018 World Cup: Modric's penalty kick seals 2–0 Croatia victory |last=Bogage |first=Jacob |date=16 June 2018 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |archive-date=19 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180619020234/https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/soccer-insider/wp/2018/06/16/croatia-vs-nigeria-2018-world-cup/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Striker [[Nikola Kalinić (footballer)|Nikola Kalinić]] refused to enter the match as a [[Substitute (association football)|substitute]], citing back pain as his reason for not playing, and was expelled from the team by manager [[Zlatko Dalić]], leaving Croatia with only 22 players for the remainder of the tournament.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/18/croatia-send-reluctant-substitute-nikola-kalinic |title=Croatia send reluctant substitute Nikola Kalinic home from World Cup |last=MacInnes |first=Paul |date=18 June 2018 |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=15 July 2018 |archive-date=15 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180715181630/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jun/18/croatia-send-reluctant-substitute-nikola-kalinic |url-status=live }}</ref> Croatia went on to beat Argentina 3–0, taking the lead in the second half when goalkeeper [[Willy Caballero]]'s attempted clearance was intercepted by [[Ante Rebić]], and then scoring twice more through Modrić and Ivan Rakitić. [[Lionel Messi]], Argentina's captain, was described by BBC Sport's Jennings as "adrift for much of the match". Croatia finished atop the group with a 2–1 win over Iceland, resting several starting players in the final group match.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44439114 |title=Argentina 0–3 Croatia |last=Jennings |first=Patrick |date=21 June 2018 |access-date=11 July 2018 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |archive-date=24 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180624184748/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44439114 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url-access=subscription |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/26/sports/world-cup/iceland-vs-croatia.html |title=Croatia Crushes Iceland's World Cup Dream |last=Mather |first=Victor |date=26 June 2018 |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |archive-date=12 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180712040204/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/26/sports/world-cup/iceland-vs-croatia.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.efe.com/efe/english/sports/croatia-take-group-d-with-2-1-win-against-iceland/50000266-3663035 |title=Croatia take Group D with 2–1 win against Iceland |date=27 June 2018 |access-date=12 July 2018 |publisher=[[EFE]] |archive-date=30 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200930084454/https://www.efe.com/efe/english/sports/croatia-take-group-d-with-2-1-win-against-iceland/50000266-3663035 |url-status=live }}</ref>

<!--[[File:Croatia's post-match huddle after the 2018 FIFA World Cup Final.jpg|thumb|The [[Template:Croatia squad 2018 FIFA World Cup|2017–18 Croatia national squad]], dubbed the "Second Golden Generation", posing with [[Vladimir Putin]] and [[Kolinda Grabar-Kitarović]] after the final against France.]]-->
In the round of 16, Croatia played Denmark on 1 July at Nizhny Novgorod Stadium.<ref>{{cite web |title=Croatia / Denmark – Scoresheet |publisher=[[Al Jazeera]] |date=1 July 2018 |access-date=26 October 2021 |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/live-scores/football/world-cup/round-of-16/match/380310/croatia-denmark |archive-date=26 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211026094411/https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/live-scores/football/world-cup/round-of-16/match/380310/croatia-denmark |url-status=live }}</ref> They earned a 1–1 draw after the two teams exchanged goals in the opening five minutes and Modrić missed a penalty in [[overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]]. Croatia won the subsequent penalty shoot-out 3–2, with three saves by goalkeeper [[Danijel Subašić]] and two saves by Danish goalkeeper [[Kasper Schmeichel]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-cup/2018/07/01/croatia-vs-denmark-world-cup-2018-live-score-latest-updates/ |title=Croatia through to World Cup quarter-finals after dramatic penalty shoot-out victory over Denmark |last1=Wilson |first1=Jeremy |date=2 July 2018 |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |last2=Eccleshare |first2=Charlie |archive-date=10 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180710222405/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-cup/2018/07/01/croatia-vs-denmark-world-cup-2018-live-score-latest-updates/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/russia2018/matches/match/300331498/#match-summary |title=Subasic the hero as Croatia claim shootout win |date=2 July 2018 |access-date=11 July 2018 |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |archive-date=24 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200224033056/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/russia2018/matches/match/300331498/#match-summary |url-status=live }}</ref> The team advanced to a quarter-final at [[Fisht Olympic Stadium]] in Sochi against hosts Russia, on 7 July.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/live-scores/football/world-cup/quarter-final/match/380306/russia-croatia |title=Russia / Croatia – Scoresheet |publisher=[[Al Jazeera]] |date=7 July 2018 |access-date=26 October 2021 |archive-date=26 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211026094411/https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/live-scores/football/world-cup/quarter-final/match/380306/russia-croatia |url-status=live }}</ref> The Russians scored their first in the 31st minute, but [[Andrej Kramarić]] equalised for Croatia eight minutes later and kept the score at 1–1 through the end of regular time. Croatia took a 2–1 lead in the 101st minute with a [[Header (association football)|header]] by [[Domagoj Vida]], but Russian defender [[Mário Fernandes]] equalised five minutes before the end of extra time to trigger a penalty shoot-out. The shoot-out was won 4–3 by Croatia after two misses by Russia and a shot by Modrić that rebounded into the goal off [[Igor Akinfeev]]'s arm and the post.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2018/07/07/russia-world-cup-croatia-penalty-kicks/765749002/ |title=Russia's underdog World Cup run comes to an end with PK loss to Croatia |last=Rogers |first=Martin |date=7 July 2018 |work=[[USA Today]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |archive-date=8 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180708083330/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2018/07/07/russia-world-cup-croatia-penalty-kicks/765749002/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jul/07/russia-croatia-world-cup-quarter-final-match-report |title=Croatia book World Cup semi-final with England after penalty shootout win |last=Hytner |first=David |date=7 July 2018 |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |archive-date=12 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180712021106/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jul/07/russia-croatia-world-cup-quarter-final-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> Croatia became the second team in World Cup to win two shoot-outs in a tournament, after Argentina in [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]].<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.espn.com/soccer/fifa-world-cup/4/blog/post/3559381/by-the-numbers-back-to-back-shootout-wins-for-croatia-record-penalty-saves-for-danijel-subasic |title=By the numbers: Back-to-back shootout wins for Croatia, record penalty saves for Subasic |last=Debayan |date=7 July 2018 |access-date=11 July 2018 |publisher=[[ESPN]] |archive-date=12 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180712024446/http://www.espn.com/soccer/fifa-world-cup/4/blog/post/3559381/by-the-numbers-back-to-back-shootout-wins-for-croatia-record-penalty-saves-for-danijel-subasic |url-status=live }}</ref> After the match, a video of Vida shouting "[[Glory to Ukraine]]" prompted controversy among Russians and a warning from FIFA's disciplinary committee, which enforces a ban on political slogans.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.espn.com/soccer/fifa-world-cup/story/3559901/croatia-defender-domagoj-vida-warned-by-fifa-over-pro-ukraine-comments |title=Croatia defender Domagoj Vida warned by FIFA over pro-Ukraine comments |last=Petrosyan |first=Artur |date=8 July 2018 |access-date=11 July 2018 |publisher=[[ESPN]] |archive-date=27 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211027084543/https://www.espn.com/soccer/fifa-world-cup/story/3559901/croatia-defender-domagoj-vida-warned-by-fifa-over-pro-ukraine-comments |url-status=live }}</ref> Croatia's semi-final match against [[England national football team|England]], on 11 July at the Luzhniki, began as they conceded a [[free kick]] goal by English defender [[Kieran Trippier]] in the fifth minute. Croatia resisted several attempts by England to score a second goal in the first half. Croatia managed an equalising goal of their own through a shot by [[Ivan Perišić]] in the 68th minute. The match was won 2–1 by Croatia after a 109th-minute goal by Mandžukić. This made Croatia the first team to earn three come-from-behind victories in the FIFA World Cup, all three matches also going into extra time.<ref>{{cite news |url-access=subscription |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/07/11/sports/world-cup/england-vs-croatia.html |title=Croatia Turns England's World Cup Destiny Into Despair |last1=Das |first1=Andrew |date=11 July 2018 |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |last2=Mather |first2=Victor |archive-date=12 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180712012041/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/07/11/sports/world-cup/england-vs-croatia.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2018/07/11/croatia-england-mario-mandzukic-world-cup-semifinals/776569002/ |title=Croatia tops England in extra time to reach World Cup final |last=Rogers |first=Martin |date=11 July 2018 |work=[[USA Today]] |access-date=11 July 2018 |archive-date=11 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711213715/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2018/07/11/croatia-england-mario-mandzukic-world-cup-semifinals/776569002/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Pre-match==
[[File:Néstor Pitana.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Néstor Pitana]] officiated the final.]]
[[Argentine Football Association|Argentine]] referee [[Néstor Pitana]] was selected to lead the officiating team for the final, which was announced on 12 July 2018 by the FIFA Referees Committee. The final was Pitana's fifth match as referee during the tournament, becoming only the second referee to officiate the opening match and the final.<ref name=":5">{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/pitana-it-s-an-indescribable-feeling |title=Pitana: It's an indescribable feeling |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=13 July 2018 |access-date=14 July 2018 |archive-date=14 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180714000650/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/pitana-it-s-an-indescribable-feeling |url-status=live }}</ref> Pitana officiated an additional group stage match, along with two knockout stage matches in the round of 16 and quarter-finals. Pitana had been a [[FIFA International Referees List|FIFA referee]] since 2010, and officiated four matches at the 2014 World Cup. His compatriots Hernán Maidana and [[Juan Pablo Belatti]] were chosen as assistant referees. [[Björn Kuipers]] of the Netherlands was chosen as the fourth official, with his fellow countryman Erwin Zeinstra as the reserve assistant.<ref name="officials">{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/nestor-pitana-to-referee-world-cup-final |title=Nestor Pitana to referee World Cup Final |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=12 July 2018 |access-date=12 July 2018 |archive-date=10 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180910211146/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/nestor-pitana-to-referee-world-cup-final |url-status=live }}</ref> Italian [[Massimiliano Irrati]] was named the video assistant referee, presiding over the first use of the technology at a World Cup final. Argentine [[Mauro Vigliano]] was chosen as the assistant video assistant referee, while Carlos Astroza of Chile was appointed as the second assistant and [[Danny Makkelie]] of the Netherlands as the third assistant.<ref>{{cite news |last=Gantman |first=Marcelo |url=https://www.lanacion.com.ar/2152543-pitana-una-roca-que-marco-el-estilo-arbitral-de-rusia-2018 |title=Los atributos que le vio la FIFA a Néstor Pitana para designarlo para la final del Mundial Rusia 2018: "la roca" que marcó el estilo arbitral |trans-title=The attributes that saw FIFA designate Néstor Pitana for the 2018 World Cup Final: "the rock" that marked the refereeing style |work=[[La Nación]] |date=12 July 2018 |access-date=12 July 2018 |language=es |archive-date=12 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180712220043/https://www.lanacion.com.ar/2152543-pitana-una-roca-que-marco-el-estilo-arbitral-de-rusia-2018 |url-status=live }}</ref>

{{anchor|Closing ceremony}}
The tournament's closing ceremony was held prior to the start of the match, featuring a performance of "[[Live It Up (Nicky Jam song)|Live It Up]]", the official song of the tournament, by [[Will Smith]], [[Nicky Jam]], and [[Era Istrefi]]. Jam also performed "[[X (Equis)]]", wearing a shirt honouring [[J Balvin]].<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.jornada.com.mx/ultimas/2018/07/15/will-smith-nicky-jam-y-era-istrefi-calientan-motores-en-rusia-5776.html |title=Will Smith, Nicky Jam y Era Istrefi calientan la final |date=15 July 2018 |work=[[La Jornada]] |access-date=15 July 2018 |agency=Notimex |language=es |trans-title=Will Smith, Nicky Jam and Era Istrefi warm up the final |archive-date=17 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180717150336/http://www.jornada.com.mx/ultimas/2018/07/15/will-smith-nicky-jam-y-era-istrefi-calientan-motores-en-rusia-5776.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> Opera singer [[Aida Garifullina]] sang the Russian folk song "[[Kalinka (1860 song)|Kalinka]]", accompanied by a children's choir and percussion section that featured a cameo by former Brazilian international [[Ronaldinho]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-worldcup-final-closing-ceremon/hollywood-star-will-smith-brings-down-curtain-on-russia-world-cup-idUSKBN1K50PG |title=Hollywood star Will Smith brings down curtain on Russia World Cup |date=15 July 2018 |access-date=15 July 2018 |publisher=[[Reuters]] |archive-date=18 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180718002844/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-worldcup-final-closing-ceremon/hollywood-star-will-smith-brings-down-curtain-on-russia-world-cup-idUSKBN1K50PG |url-status=live }}</ref> There were ten [[heads of state]] in attendance, among them Russian president [[Vladimir Putin]], French president [[Emmanuel Macron]], and Croatian president [[Kolinda Grabar-Kitarović]].<ref name="WaPo2">{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/soccer-insider/wp/2018/07/15/france-vs-croatia-2018-world-cup-final/ |title=France blazes past Croatia to win World Cup title for the second time |last1=Goff |first1=Steven |date=15 July 2018 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |access-date=15 July 2018 |last2=Fortier |first2=Sam |last3=Wilson |first3=Scott |archive-date=15 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180715165709/https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/soccer-insider/wp/2018/07/15/france-vs-croatia-2018-world-cup-final/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

The starting line-ups for both teams were identical to those fielded in the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/world-cup-final-2018-france-vs-croatia-team-news-line-ups-starting-xi-mbappe-pogba-video-watch-a8448266.html |title=World Cup final: France and Croatia name unchanged line-ups as Kylian Mbappe starts |last=Austin |first=Jack |date=15 July 2018 |work=[[The Independent]] |access-date=15 July 2018 |archive-date=15 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180715174757/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/world-cup-final-2018-france-vs-croatia-team-news-line-ups-starting-xi-mbappe-pogba-video-watch-a8448266.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Deschamps chose a [[4–4–2 formation]] while his opposite number Dalić opted for a [[4–1–4–1]] with Mandžukić as the lone [[Striker (association football)|striker]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=France beat Croatia in World Cup final |last=McNulty |first=Phil |author-link=Phil McNulty |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=15 July 2018 |access-date=25 October 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44754965 |archive-date=25 March 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190325011256/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44754965 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==
===First half===
[[File:Final of the Soccer World Cup Russia between the national teams of France and Croatia.jpg|left|thumb|Teams lined up prior to kick-off]]
Croatia kicked off the match at 18:00 local time (15:00 [[UTC]]) in temperatures of {{convert|27|°C|°F|abbr=on}} with 51% humidity, in front of a crowd of 78,011 and an estimated global television audience of 1.12 billion.<ref name="FRA-CRO line-ups"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=Around half the world's population tuned in to this year's soccer World Cup |last=Clinch |first=Matt |work=[[CNBC]] |date=21 December 2018 |access-date=20 October 2021 |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/12/21/world-cup-2018-half-the-worlds-population-tuned-in-to-this-years-soccer-tournament.html |quote=1.12 billion |archive-date=20 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211020183147/https://www.cnbc.com/2018/12/21/world-cup-2018-half-the-worlds-population-tuned-in-to-this-years-soccer-tournament.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The weather at [[Sheremetyevo International Airport]], {{convert|30|km|mi|sigfig=2}} from the stadium,{{efn|Distance measured using Google Maps distance calculator, between Sheremetyevo – A.S. Pushkin international airport, coordinates 55.9736512°N, 37.4103143°E and Luzhniki Stadium, coordinates 55.715765°N, 37.551527°E.<ref>{{Google maps |url=https://www.google.com/maps/place/Sheremetyevo+-+A.S.+Pushkin+international+airport/@55.9736512,37.4103143,17z |access-date=20 October 2021 |title=Sheremetyevo – A.S. Pushkin international airport}}</ref><ref>{{Google maps |url=https://www.google.co.uk/maps/place/Luzhniki+Stadium/@55.715765,37.551527,17z |access-date=20 October 2021 |title=Luzhniki Stadium}}</ref>}} was recorded as partly cloudy at the time of kick-off.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Moscow, Moscow, Russia Weather History – Jul 15, 2018 |publisher=[[The Weather Company]] |date= |access-date=20 October 2021 |url=https://www.wunderground.com/history/daily/ru/moscow/UUEE/date/2018-7-15 |archive-date=20 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211020115930/https://www.wunderground.com/history/daily/ru/moscow/UUEE/date/2018-7-15 |url-status=live }}</ref> The match was played through a minor thunderstorm, which produced several visible lightning strikes.<ref name="NYT-Live2">{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/07/15/sports/world-cup/france-vs-croatia-final.html |url-access=subscription |title=France vs. Croatia: World Cup Final Live Updates |last1=Das |first1=Andrew |date=15 July 2018 |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=15 July 2018 |last2=Mather |first2=Victor |archive-date=11 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191211172908/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/07/15/sports/world-cup/france-vs-croatia-final.html |url-status=live }}</ref> ''The Guardian''{{'s}} [[Barry Glendenning]] wrote that Croatia had the better of the first 15 minutes, noting that France were "struggling to get out of their own half" and that Croatia were "bossing them completely." However, France took the lead in the 18th minute. They won a free kick around {{convert|30|yards|m}} from goal when [[Marcelo Brozović]] fouled Griezmann, which Griezmann took himself. He crossed the free kick into the penalty area, where it reached Mandžukić.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> He attempted to head clear, but the ball instead went past goalkeeper Subašić for an own goal, the first ever own goal in a World Cup final.<ref name="Telegraph-Live2">{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-cup/2018/07/15/france-vs-croatia-world-cup-final-2018-live-score-latest-updates/ |title=World Cup final 2018, France vs Croatia: live score and latest updates |last=Bull |first=JJ |date=15 July 2018 |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |access-date=15 July 2018 |archive-date=15 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180715211043/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-cup/2018/07/15/france-vs-croatia-world-cup-final-2018-live-score-latest-updates/ |url-status=live }}</ref> [[N'Golo Kanté]] received the game's first [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] in the 28th minute for a foul on Perišić. Croatia equalised a minute later, when Perišić was fouled {{convert|40|yards|m}} from goal and Modrić's free kick towards the far goalpost was headed back across goal by [[Šime Vrsaljko]] and collected by Perišić, who hit a low shot into the corner of the goal.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2018 final: France 4–2 Croatia – as it happened |last1=Glendenning |first1=Barry |last2=Unwin |first2=Will |last3=McVeigh |first3=Niall |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=15 July 2018 |access-date=24 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2018/jul/15/world-cup-2018-final-france-v-croatia-live?page=with:block-5b4b7806e4b0f86cea749cd8#liveblog-navigation |archive-date=24 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211024131711/https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2018/jul/15/world-cup-2018-final-france-v-croatia-live?page=with:block-5b4b7806e4b0f86cea749cd8#liveblog-navigation |url-status=live }}</ref>

Five minutes after Croatia's goal, Griezmann took a corner into the penalty area. [[Blaise Matuidi]]'s attempt to score with a header was not successful, but France players appealed for a penalty, claiming that Perišić had handled the ball while [[Marking (association football)|marking]] Matuidi. The video assistant referee alerted Pitana and after he reviewed the incident for several minutes, he gave a penalty to France. The penalty was taken by Griezmann in the 38th minute, and he scored with a low kick into the left-hand corner. In the 43rd minute, [[Lucas Hernandez]] was booked for a foul on Rebić and then Perišić had a shot on goal which was deflected for a corner by [[Paul Pogba]]. Shortly before half-time, Croatia had a corner which was taken by Rakitić into the penalty area, where Vida had an opportunity to score with his head, but the ball went wide of the goal.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> The first half finished with France leading 2–1, despite having only one shot on goal and 34% of the possession.<ref name="Telegraph-Live2"/> During the half-time break, pundits discussed the legitimacy of both France's goals, with [[Alan Shearer]] and [[Rio Ferdinand]] on [[BBC One]] saying that neither the free kick which led to their first goal, nor the penalty for the second, should have been awarded.<ref name="VARPenaltyBBC"/> Shearer labelled the penalty decision "ridiculous", saying that in his opinion there was "no way that the hand-ball was deliberate".<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup final: Alan Shearer blasts 'ridiculous' VAR decision for France penalty |last=Hanna |first=Gareth |work=[[Belfast Telegraph]] |date=15 July 2018 |access-date=25 October 2021 |url=https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/world-cup-final-alan-shearer-blasts-ridiculous-var-decision-for-france-penalty-37120467.html |archive-date=27 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211027084644/https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/world-cup-final-alan-shearer-blasts-ridiculous-var-decision-for-france-penalty-37120467.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Chris Waddle]], speaking on [[BBC Radio 5 Live]] thought the penalty decision was correct, however, saying "I would have given it. Perišić has stopped the ball going through with his hand."<ref name="VARPenaltyBBC">{{Cite web |title=VAR penalty: Ridiculous? Right decision? |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=15 July 2018 |access-date=25 October 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44841143 |archive-date=8 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211008051430/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44841143 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Gabriele Marcotti]] of [[ESPN]] described it as "the sort of decision that, even after replay, could have gone either way".<ref name="ESPNThrilling">{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/football/fifa-world-cup/4/blog/post/3566257/thrilling-world-cup-gets-finale-it-deserves-as-france-end-croatia-dream-run |publisher=[[ESPN]] |title=Thrilling World Cup gets fitting finale as France end Croatia's dream run |date=15 July 2018 |first=Gabriele |last=Marcotti |access-date=25 October 2021 |archive-date=25 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211025160622/https://www.espn.co.uk/football/fifa-world-cup/4/blog/post/3566257/thrilling-world-cup-gets-finale-it-deserves-as-france-end-croatia-dream-run |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Second half===
[[File:Antoine Griezmann World Cup Trophy.jpg|upright|thumb|[[Antoine Griezmann]] was named the man of the match after scoring one goal and assisting another.]]
A Croatian attack was stopped early in the second half after several [[pitch invader]]s ran onto the field before being removed by security officers; Russian feminist rock band and protest group [[Pussy Riot]] claimed responsibility for the interruption.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/worldcup/pussy-riot-claim-responsibility-for-world-cup-final-pitch-invasion-a3887696.html |title=Pussy Riot claim responsibility for World Cup Final pitch invasion |date=15 July 2018 |work=[[Evening Standard]] |access-date=16 July 2018 |archive-date=15 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180715210723/https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/worldcup/pussy-riot-claim-responsibility-for-world-cup-final-pitch-invasion-a3887696.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Three minutes into the second half, Croatia had an opportunity to score when Rebić struck the ball towards goal from the left side of the penalty area following a pass by Rakitić. French goalkeeper [[Hugo Lloris]] made a one-handed save from his shot, sending the ball behind for a corner.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="ESPNThrilling"/> Glendenning said that Croatia had begun the second half as they began the first, "in a state of total dominance".<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> France had an opportunity in the 52nd minute, when Mbappé received the ball from Pogba and ran down the left-hand side. He ran past Vida and shot towards goal, but Subašić was able to claim the ball. France made a substitution in the 55th minute when [[Steven Nzonzi]] replaced Kanté.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> They then increased their lead in the 59th minute, when Pogba found Mbappé again, on the right wing, with a pass from his own half.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Mbappé passed to Griezmann, who then sent the ball back to Pogba who was outside the penalty area. Pogba took a shot at goal which rebounded back to him off a defender, before he sent a second shot into the Croatian goal with Subašić unable to reach it.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="ESPNThrilling"/>

France had a chance to extend their lead in the 63rd minute when [[Olivier Giroud]] hit the ball with a [[bicycle kick]] towards Griezmann, who was not marked in front of goal, but Brozović was able to gain possession before the ball reached Griezmann. Two minutes later, they did score again when Mbappé hit a shot from {{convert|25|yards|m}} which went past Vida and Subašić, low into the Croatian goal.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Mbappé became only the second teenager to score in a World Cup final, the first being [[Pelé]] when he scored twice [[1958 FIFA World Cup final|in 1958]].<ref>{{Cite web |title='Welcome to the club' – Pele praises Kylian Mbappe for becoming the second teenager to score in a World Cup final |last=Twomey |first=Liam |work=[[The Independent]] |date=15 July 2018 |access-date=25 October 2021 |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/world-cup-2018-france-croatia-pele-kylian-mbappe-welcome-club-teenage-scorer-goal-video-a8448611.html#r3z-addoor |archive-date=16 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180716153330/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/world-cup-2018-france-croatia-pele-kylian-mbappe-welcome-to-the-club-teenage-scorer-goal-video-a8448611.html#r3z-addoor |url-status=live }}</ref> Croatia scored their second goal in the 69th minute from a back-pass that Lloris failed to dribble away from Mandžukić, who poked the loose ball into the unguarded net with his right leg. Despite late pressure by Croatia, the match finished as a 4–2 victory for France, the highest-scoring World Cup final since [[1966 FIFA World Cup final|1966]] and the first World Cup final since [[2002 FIFA World Cup final|2002]] to be decided without extra time.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="Reuters-Summary2">{{cite news |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-worldcup-final/france-lift-second-world-cup-after-winning-classic-final-4-2-idUSKBN1K50RG |title=France lift second World Cup after winning classic final 4–2 |date=15 July 2018 |access-date=15 July 2018 |publisher=[[Reuters]] |archive-date=2 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181202080047/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-worldcup-final/france-lift-second-world-cup-after-winning-classic-final-4-2-idUSKBN1K50RG |url-status=live }}</ref>

